{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 names 'integration' but reduces to the same \"removes trace → still detectable\" obstacle that SRC_004 raises; it never identifies a distinct integrative mechanism (e.g., a shared interface or dependency between the isolation method and the blind-spot theory). Because the refutation hinges only on residual fingerprints re-triggering the checker, the stated relation is interchangeable with incompatibility, so the 'integration' label is unsupported by the content.","counterexample":[{"content":"Stateless isolation (SRC_002) depends on the 'blind spot correlation' theory (SRC_005): it assumes removal of the authoring trace is sufficient only because the checker's latent path is keyed to that trace; refuted if fingerprints persist through the wipe and still key the same path.","role":"relation-is-dependence-not-integration"}],"target":"3fd000087ac7d5d2e6235a9777d77f7421dc8afc6563443076b88008c3314a10"},{"attack":false,"case":"","target":"5ce94699cd45afeb2a97a9096f35f8e72a6f62b2b7663d47123dc8f0a7dd7a0b"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_003 treats 'blind spot correlation' and 'separate engines' as interchangeable: it says the necessity of separate engines abstracts the blind-spot problem, but its refutation only shows a single engine might bypass probabilistic failure modes via deterministic execution. That rebuts the separate-engine requirement without establishing that the blind-spot phenomenon itself is what is being abstracted; the target conflates an architectural claim with the error-mode claim it is supposedly abstracting.","counterexample":[{"content":"The necessity of separate engines (SRC_005) abstracts the blind-spot correlation problem into an architectural redundancy requirement; refuted if a single engine, using deterministic symbolic execution, eliminates the blind-spot correlation itself — not merely probabilistic failure modes generally.","role":"refutation-must-target-the-abstracted-property-not-a-proxy"}],"target":"8a3306fd9229f2b08bbbd4e4cf3727e87d81f09af7bd72f974f73a25336f76d9"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_04's 'compatibility' is conditional and thin: it says separation is unnecessary is compatible with the blind-spot theory only if adversarial-mode paths are statistically orthogonal to authoring paths, then refutes via sycophancy. But sycophancy (confirming prior outputs) is not the same failure as non-orthogonality of adversarial vs authoring paths; the refutation must show the adversarial mode traverses overlapping paths, not merely that it is biased toward self-confirmation. The stated refuter does not match the condition it negates.","counterexample":[{"content":"Compatibility is refuted if the adversarial mode, despite orthogonal path activation, still inherits sycophantic bias — because orthogonality of activated paths and bias toward confirming prior outputs are independent failure axes, so one cannot refute the other.","role":"refuter-must-negate-the-stated-condition"}],"target":"defb53c347187a58e80e81afac5477df8e8d8d4f342263255af8cd389ed183f3"}]}